    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHAT THIS DOES NOT REST ON. The fact is deliberately independent of the COMPLETENESS half of Armstrong's theorem. The implied direction needs only SOUNDNESS of the three axioms, which is what makes a replayed derivation mean something. The unimplied direction needs no metatheorem at all: a two-row relation satisfying F and violating X -> Y is a semantic counterexample, so it refutes the implication directly rather than by appeal to a completeness result. Completeness is what guarantees such a relation always EXISTS when the implication fails; here one is exhibited, so the guarantee is not needed.\n\nIt does rest on one thing the classical statement leaves implicit and this ledger should not: every attribute domain must have at least two values, or a two-row counterexample is not constructible. That is in the axiom footprint.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
